# ThreadSanitizer suppressions — THIRD-PARTY CODE ONLY.
#
# Rule: every entry here must be scoped to a shared object Lumen does not build.
# Never suppress by function name (`race_top:pthread_mutex_destroy` and friends), because
# Lumen's own std::mutex lowers to exactly those symbols — a real "destroy while another
# thread holds it" bug in Lumen would vanish along with the driver's.
#
# ── libvulkan_lvp.so (Mesa lavapipe, the software Vulkan ICD) ────────────────────────
#
# On teardown lavapipe calls pthread_mutex_destroy / pthread_cond_destroy on primitives
# its own llvmpipe worker threads are still locking and broadcasting on. TSan reports it
# as a data race on the mutex/cond object; it is a genuine race, but it is Mesa's, inside
# Mesa's threads, on Mesa's objects.
#
# Verified before suppressing: across 12 runs of test_dmabuf_composite, every racing
# access frame (#0 an intercepted libc call, #1 the driver) resolved to libvulkan_lvp.so.
# Zero Lumen frames appeared in any racing access — Lumen shows up only at #2+ in the
# thread-creation stack, because `gfx::device_selector::select_best()` is what caused the
# driver to spawn those threads in the first place.
#
# Scope note: `race:` matches any frame in the report, so this also hides a hypothetical
# Lumen race on memory the driver touches. That is the narrowest form that works —
# `called_from_lib:` does not suppress these (the reports come from TSan's pthread
# interceptors, not from instrumented driver code), verified 4/4 runs still failing.
#
# Reproduce the underlying reports with:
#   TSAN_OPTIONS= ./build/tsan/test_dmabuf_composite
race:libvulkan_lvp.so
